Recover Codex after its reconnect retries expire

Codex 0.163.0 leaves its frontend alive after its 120-second reconnect
budget expires. Rumpelpod must replace that exhausted frontend after
connectivity returns instead of reattaching users to a stranded UI.

Watch the parsed terminal's failure status, including the agent command
center presentation, while excluding ordinary draft and transcript text.
Wait for the remote Codex app-server to answer initialize before
replacing the local frontend. Short outages remain Codex's
responsibility.

Keep attached clients connected across PTY replacement and recover
detached sessions without a new attachment. Serialize replacement with
lifecycle cleanup and reject monitors belonging to an obsolete session
generation. Resume the thread observed in successful start, resume, and
fork responses; do not replay launch arguments that can contain an
initial prompt. Unsent drafts are intentionally discarded on
replacement.

Integration coverage uses the real Codex version pinned by the
devcontainer and a nested SSH host. Short outages preserve the original
frontend and draft; long outages replace it, restore the conversation,
and accept a new turn with clients either attached or detached. Only
inference uses the existing LLM cache; transport, terminal rendering,
retry timing, and process lifecycle are real.

Run the recovery cases with:
  cargo pipeline codex_frontend_recovers_after_ssh_retry_exhaustion

The upstream behavior was confirmed in rust-v0.163.0 at
83086188ae0614bd882818ff1aa23f3194962012 and main at
46d252f8bf8b09f4ef765bc72e40f02c229da3e6. Codex exposes no supported
frontend retry-exhaustion notification, so the terminal banner is a
version-sensitive compatibility boundary covered by the tests.
